 <div class="mp3-usecases-header">
 <span class="section-eyebrow text-caption-mono">Troubleshooting</span>
 <h2 id="mp3-troubleshoot-headline" class="section-headline">
 Common problems and solutions.
 </h2>
 </div>

 <div class="mp3-usecases-grid">
 <article class="mp3-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="mp3-usecase-title">"Audio won't download."</h3>
 <p class="mp3-usecase-body">
 Nine times out of ten, the TikTok link is wrong. Someone copied a link from a comment instead of the actual video, or the URL got mangled during copy-paste. Go back to TikTok, open the video itself, tap Share → Copy Link, and paste that fresh URL. If the video was deleted by the creator, no converter can retrieve it. Not TikSaveHub, not anyone else.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="mp3-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="mp3-usecase-title">"Quality is low."</h3>
 <p class="mp3-usecase-body">
 Quality depends on what TikTok compressed the audio to. Most TikTok videos carry 128kbps AAC audio. TikSaveHub converts that losslessly to MP3. It can't improve what was already compressed. If the audio sounds thin on TikTok, it'll sound thin in the MP3 too. That's a source-quality issue, not a tool limitation.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="mp3-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="mp3-usecase-title">"Link isn't working."</h3>
 <p class="mp3-usecase-body">
 TikSaveHub handles all standard TikTok link formats, but it only works with video URLs. Profile pages, hashtag pages, and search results aren't downloadable. They don't point to a specific video. The link needs to contain /video/, vm.tiktok.com, or vt.tiktok.com.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="mp3-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="mp3-usecase-title">"Conversion takes too long."</h3>
 <p class="mp3-usecase-body">
 The conversion itself is fast. Under three seconds. The bottleneck is file transfer. A 60-second TikTok video's audio is typically 1 to 2MB as MP3. On a 10 Mbps connection, that's under two seconds of download time. On slower mobile data, it takes longer. Switch to Wi-Fi if it's dragging.
 </p>
 </article>

 <article class="mp3-usecase-card">
 <h3 class="mp3-usecase-title">"No audio in the file."</h3>
 <p class="mp3-usecase-body">
 Rare, but it happens. Usually when TikSaveHub can't locate the audio track in the video (some TikTok videos have unusual encoding). Wait a few minutes and try again. If the problem persists, the video may have been restricted on TikTok's end. Try a different video to confirm the tool is working.
 </p>
 </article>
 </div>
 </div>
